
What does "Generic" mean in Java?

Generics in Java allow you to define classes, interfaces, and methods with a placeholder for a data type, rather
than using a fixed type or raw type. This tells Java ahead of time (at compile time) what type you're working with,
providing type safety and eliminating the need for casting.

Why use Generics?

Type Safety: You get compile-time checks to avoid ClassCastException.
Code Reusability: You can write one class/method that works with any data type.
Avoid Casting: No need to manually cast objects from Object.


We can define any collection without specifying the generic types, but it may lead to ClassCastException at runtime.

In Java, generics provide compile-time type safety. If you skip the generic type, the compiler treats the collection as a raw type, meaning it does not enforce any type constraints.

❗ Risk of not using generics:

Lack of type safety
Runtime errors (ClassCastException)
Harder to maintain code

Note : Raw types are discouraged and only allowed for backward compatibility. Using raw types disables generic type checks at compile-time.